    "knowledge_point": "Solving for an Unknown Using the Law of Total Probability",
    "problem": "Daniel has a desk with two drawers, drawer $A$ and drawer $B.$ Of the total amount of pens that he has, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of them are in drawer $A,$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ are in drawer $B.$ Also, $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the pens in drawer $A$ are blue, and Daniel knows that $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the pens in both drawers are blue. What is the probability that a randomly selected pen from drawer $B$ is blue?",
    "worked_solution": "Let $U$ be the event that the chosen pen is blue, and let $A$ be the event that the pen was selected from drawer $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the pen was selected from drawer $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pen is blue, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(U)=P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{4}{5}$ of the pens are in drawer $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ are in drawer $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{4}{5},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the pens in drawer $A$ are blue, we have\n\n$$\nP(U|A)=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{2}{3}$ of the pens on the desk drawers are blue, we have\n\n$$\nP(U)=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(U|{D}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U) & =P(U|A)P(A)+P(U|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5})+P(U|{A}^{′})(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}P(U|{A}^{′}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}P(U|{A}^{′}) & =\\frac{10-9}{15} \\\\ P(U|{A}^{′}) & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected pen from drawer $B$ is blue is $\\frac{1}{3}.$",
